  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure builds into the region today with seasonable\ntemperatures and mostly sunny skies. \n\nThe next system to impact the area will be on Thursday. Warm\nsouthwesterly return flow will increase moisture and\ntemperatures out ahead of an approaching cold front Thursday. A\nfew showers will be possible as a warm front glides through from\nsouthwest to northeast on Thursday morning. There should be\nplenty of time for instability to build up by Thursday afternoon\nafter early morning showers. Latest model soundings are showing\ndecent instability with CAPE of 1500+ J/kg and 30+ knots of 0-6\nkm bulk shear. Timing of the surface cold front is looking to be\nlate afternoon to early Thursday evening, so the timing should\nmaximize instability during the peak heating of the afternoon.\nSPC currently has a slight risk for severe thunderstorms across\na large portion of the CWA. \n\nWPC has also highlighted our area under a marginal risk for\nflash flooding. Overall the threat for flash flooding is \nlooking low. PWATs are not too out of the ordinary, at only 1 to\n2 standard deviations above normal and storms should be \nprogressive. \n\nShowers may linger into the overnight hours Thursday into Friday\nmorning as the front slowly pushes through and exits the region\nearly Friday morning. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure builds in behind the exiting cold front on Friday.\nThis should make for an excellent weekend with highs in the mid\nto upper 70s on Saturday and into the low 80s on Sunday. The\nnext chance for showers will come on Monday as another front\nsags south out of Canada.",
